Git registers linked worktrees under '.git/worktrees/<name>/'. Each entry
holds the administrative state: the private index, HEAD, and path.

Removing a worktree directory with 'rm -rf' leaves Git's administrative
records behind as orphans. 'git worktree prune' scans the registered
paths and deletes records whose directories have vanished.

However, if a worktree resides on a removable flash drive or network
filesystem, unmounting it makes the directory appear gone. An unguided
prune would wipe its administrative state.

Locking the worktree prevents prune from clearing its record while the
filesystem is absent.
